#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pin — mint a durable fingerprint address; resolve it onto a later tree.

A file:line is a locator. It dies when the file splits. A pin is the
fingerprint (line + identifiers + in-scope neighbors + path hint +
optional repo origin) so a ticket can store the address without the SHA.

v0.4 keeps the v0.3 refuses and flips the leftover-stub overcorrection:
  * leftover stubs at the old path are not identity
  * extract-and-keep is not a move — if the origin function still holds
    the implementation body, that path is the locus (not a stem-split copy).
    Comments that push the body past --context are not a leftover stub.
  * unique means unique in both snapshots
  * files over 1 MB still mint and resolve
  * NFC and NFD path bytes are one path
  * a missing --to ref is an error, not a deletion
  * a truncated token fails closed
  * a pin does not land on a foreign repo unless --any-repo
"""
from __future__ import annotations

